  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this morning shows a large ridge of high \npressure still dominating the synoptic pattern across much of the \neastern US as a trough approaching from the PNW looks to bring \nchange to the central US. The first influences from the trough - now \nsituated near the WY/ID border - are noted with scattered showers \nand storms across much of the northern Plains as ample PVA streams \nin within the southwesterly flow. Through the day today, a shortwave \njust ahead of the main trough axis will push out into the central \nPlains. A deepened lee trough axis will follow suit as a weak \nboundary extending from southwestern Kansas to eastern Nebraska \nmoves east into eastern Kansas by the early afternoon. Given warm \nsector parameters of 1500-2500 J/kg of MLCAPE, 25-35 knots of \neffective shear and steep 0-3km lapse rates, storms - some strong to \nsevere - will be expected to develop along the frontal boundary and \ntrough axis around peak heating as inhibition reaches a diurnal \nminimum. Initial storm hazards should initially be hail up to 1.25\" \nand wind gusts of 60-65 mph while storms stay mostly isolated. CAMs \ndepict storm interactions later in the afternoon and evening to \ncongeal convection. This would in turn evolve storm threats to more \nof a wind threat with some isolated small hail embedded in stronger \ncores. In addition to the hail and wind threat, PWATs near the 99th \npercentile for this time of year may lead to torrential rainfall and \nisolated flooding. HREF guidance depicts this in latest runs with \npockets of 1.5-3\" of rainfall in the course of 3 hours. The severe \nthreat should end by the mid to late evening tonight as storms move \neast into Missouri and the effective surface boundary slides south \nof the area. \n\nScattered storm and rain chances will persist into Sunday as the \nmain trough axis ejects off the Rockies and moves across Nebraska. \nThis will come with a push of cool air from the north and will pave \nthe way for a nice week of more seasonable weather. Expect \ntemperatures for much of the upcoming work week to top out in the \n60s/70s with morning lows in the 50s/60s. Dry weather should persist \nuntil Wednesday night/Thursday when low-level flow returns back \ntowards the south and surges moisture back into the area. Luckily, \nlong range guidance keeps a fairly active jet stream pattern across \nmuch of the US that should keep temperatures on the cooler side for \nthe remainder of the month.",
